# Anonymize a series and convert it to NIfTI, then take the feed away again.
#
# This is the workflow the exemplars README called TS-only: "static scripts
# cannot capture ids between commands, so the full feed+dcm2niix workflow is
# TS-only". They can now — a run WAITS for its outcome, `${run}` and
# `${run.place}` name what it produced, and `expect` refuses when a claim
# does not hold, so the workflow is a test without a program around it.
#
# Two things are deliberate. The conversion runs on the ANONYMIZED node's
# output, never on the input, so the manifest cannot quietly convert
# identified data. And the anonymization is proved by reading the tags back
# off the files it produced, which is the only check that survives the
# plugin changing behind us.
#
# It leaves nothing behind: the feed it makes is the last thing it removes.
@name         anonymize and convert
@description  Anonymize a DICOM series, convert the result to NIfTI, verify both, clean up.
@param        SERIES
@param        SUBJECT  = anon-001
@param        LABEL    = manifest battery · anonymize and convert
@param        ANON     = pl-pfdicom_tagSub-v3.3.4
@param        CONVERT  = pl-dcm2niix-v1.0.2

# Root a feed on the series and strip the identifying elements. The tag list
# is named here because a manifest that anonymizes should say what it changed.
cd ${SERIES}
${ANON} --fileFilter dcm --tagInfo 'PatientName,${SUBJECT}++PatientID,${SUBJECT}++AccessionNumber,anon++PatientBirthDate,19000101' -- feed_title="${LABEL}"
expect run ${run} status eq finishedSuccessfully
expect path ${run.place} count --deep gt 0

# Prove it on the data, not on the plugin's word. A tag that varies across
# the folder answers with every value it takes, so one missed instance fails.
# The claims look BENEATH the node's folder: pfdicom files its output under
# `share/incoming/<input tree>`, and a claim that only looked at the top
# would be false about a run that worked.
expect path ${run.place} tags PatientID eq ${SUBJECT}
expect path ${run.place} tags PatientName eq ${SUBJECT}

# Convert what came out of the anonymizer, never what went into it.
cd ${run.place}
${CONVERT}
expect run ${run} status eq finishedSuccessfully
expect path ${run.place} count --matching '*.nii*' --deep gt 0

# Residue-free: the feed goes away with everything in it. Step out of it
# first — a workflow that deletes the place it is standing in leaves the
# session in a hole, and the next thing to run finds a cwd that is gone.
cd ~
feed rm ${feed} -f
